{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#adjunct-position-in-the-department-of-art-art-history-design": {
      "title": "Adjunct Position in the Department of Art, Art History & Design, Part-time",
      "description": "The University of Alabama in Huntsville invites applications for adjunct positions within the Department of Art, Art History & Design. Specifically, positions are available in Graphic Design and Studio Art. These positions are part-time faculty positions, hired on an as-needed basis. Compensation is based on a standard university scale according to the number of credit hours being taught. Primary responsibilities include in-person or online, asynchronous instruction based on the given student learning objective for the course, designing and implementing quality assessments, and grading students on their ability to master the learning objectives. Qualifications: Minimum requirements include a Master of Fine Arts degree in Design or Visual Arts. Previous experience as an instructor is preferred. Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae, three professional references (including name, title, email address, and phone number), and unofficial transcripts. If you are selected as a candidate for consideration by the department/committee, your references may be asked to submit a reference letter on your behalf, and you will be asked to provide official transcripts. Submit your application materials to Jill Johnson, Department Chair, at johnsok1@uah.edu . Questions or concerns should also be sent to this same email address."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#assistant-professor-of-exercise-science-tenure-track": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR OF EXERCISE SCIENCE (TENURE TRACK), COLLEGE OF EDUCATION, SPORT AND HUMAN SCIENCES",
      "description": "About the Position: The Department of Kinesiology at UAH is seeking a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Exercise Science. Duties include teaching 9 hours per semester, engaging in scholarly activities, professional service, and serving on departmental, college, and university-wide committees. This tenure-track faculty position has a strong focus on teaching and mentoring of undergraduate research. Areas of expertise may include human performance assessment, strength and conditioning, exercise physiology, biomechanics, and sports science. Primary teaching responsibilities include KIN 327: Exercise Physiology, KIN 351: Exercise Testing and Prescription, KIN 375: Strength Training and Conditioning, and KIN 451/452: Research I & II in Exercise Science. Additional responsibilities include curriculum development, laboratory supervision, and performing other duties as assigned by the Dean of the College of Education, Sport, and Human Sciences and the Kinesiology Department Chair. The ideal candidate will be committed to effective classroom teaching, student mentoring, curriculum development, and professional scholarly activity. Required Qualifications: Earned doctorate (ABD will be considered) in Exercise Science, Sports Science, Health and Human Performance, or related field Experience teaching university students in Exercise Science, Sports Science, Health and Human Performance, or related discipline Demonstrated evidence of scholarly productivity (e.g., peer-reviewed manuscripts, presentations, external funding) Demonstrated evidence to pursue a defined line of research in Exercise Science, Sports Science or Kinesiology-related field Preferred Qualifications: Experience developing/enhancing undergraduate exercise science, kinesiology, and/or sports science-related courses Experience teaching university students in online, hybrid, and/or face-to-face modalities Potential to collaborate in regards to teaching or academic research with other academic units, colleagues within the university, and community partners Demonstrated potential in grant writing and history of seeking/obtaining funded projects Knowledge of maintaining and use of human performance laboratory equipment (EMG, force plates, metabolic cart, etc.) Active participation in and certification from pertinent professional organizations (ACSM, NSCA, etc.) Experience working with programmatic accreditation Rank and Salary: This is a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor. Salary will be competitive and commensurate to rank, experience, and qualifications. An excellent and comprehensive benefits package is available. Appointment Date: Anticipated start date is Fall 2027 with the potential for Spring 2027 depending on program needs and candidate availability. About the Department: The Department of Kinesiology is authorized to award a Bachelor of Science degree in Kinesiology with concentrations in Exercise Science and Physical Education, as well as a Bachelor of Science degree in Sports & Fitness Management. The Exercise Science concentration aligns with the standards of the American College of Sports Medicine and is also an Education Recognized Program through the National Strength and Conditioning Association. About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Interested candidates should submit a letter of interest (relating your experiences to the required/preferred qualifications and responsibilities of the position), curriculum vitae, unofficial transcripts, research statement, teaching philosophy, and three current letters of recommendation. Please send application materials to: Rae Gill c/o UAH Kinesiology – Faculty Search 301 Sparkman Drive – Wilson Hall 329 Huntsville, AL 35899 Digital submissions may be sent via e-mail to rae.gill@uah.edu. Please direct any questions related to the position to Dr. Alexander Sarazen at aws0015@uah.edu. Please refer to the log number 27-28-644 when applying."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#assistant-professor-tenure-earning-in-special-education": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR (TENURE-EARNING) IN SPECIAL EDUCATION, COLLEGE OF EDUCATION, SPORT AND HUMAN SCIENCES",
      "description": "A bout the Position: The University of Alabama in Huntsville College of Education, Sport and Human Sciences is seeking a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Special Education. The ideal candidate will be well grounded in the knowledge and pedagogy of special education for students PreK-12 exhibiting a range of disabilities. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain scholarly activities, teach courses in special education and engage in school, college, university, and national service. Responsibilities of the Position: Teach undergraduate and graduate courses specifically in special education as well as other foundational courses for both the undergraduate and graduate programs within the Department of Curriculum and Instruction; Supervise and mentor students during their internship; Collaborate with colleagues to conduct program/course planning and revision; Engage in scholarly endeavors, including presentations and refereed publications; Provide academic advisement and mentoring; Provide service to the University, College, Community, and Profession Minimum Qualifications: An earned doctorate in Special Education Three (3) years minimum of instructional experience with students PreK-12 in special education settings Preferred Qualifications: BCBA Expertise in Autism and Special Education Evidence of scholarship (published, in press, or submitted, and presentations). Demonstrated potential in grant writing and implementation. Experience teaching university students in online, hybrid, and/or face-to-face models. Demonstrated ability to work effectively with students and colleagues with broadly diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds in educational, research, or other work activities. Experience in teaching and service with diverse populations and/or in urban settings with a variety of ages, both typically developing children as well as those with developmental delays and disabilities. Demonstrate an understanding of issues of importance to special education and administration, education PK-12, and teacher preparation and professional development. Potential for collaboration with university and community partners. Rank and Salary: This is a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor. Salary will be competitive and commensurate to rank, experience, and qualifications. An excellent comprehensive benefits package is available. A ppointment Date: Spring 2027; Fall 2027 considered About the College: The College of Education, Sport and Human Sciences is comprised of the Department of Curriculum and Instruction and the Department of Kinesiology.  The College offers both undergraduate and graduate degrees in teacher education and a Bachelor of Science in Kinesiology with concentrations in exercise science, physical education, and sport and fitness management.  The Educator Preparation Program serves undergraduate and graduate candidates in the areas of Biology, Chemistry, Collaborative Education (special education), Early Childhood Education/Early Childhood Special Education, Elementary Education, English Language Arts, General Science, General Social Sciences, History, Mathematics, Music Education, Orientation and Mobility, Physical Education, Physics, Visual Arts, Visual Impairments, and World Languages.  The College also offers a Master of Arts in Teaching (5 th year Alternative Master’s initial license), a Master of Education degree with concentrations in Autism Spectrum Disorders, Elementary Education, Secondary Education, English Speakers of Other Languages (ESOL), and a Master of Science degree in Applied Behavior Analysis. All programs have achieved regional and national recognition for excellence and are approved by the Alabama State Department of Education and accredited by CAEP.  For detailed information about the Teacher Education Program at UAH, visit the UAH College of Education, Sport, and Human Sciences Website . About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Applications will be reviewed as received.  Applications will be accepted until the position is filled.  Interested candidates should submit a cover letter of interest (relating your experiences to the required and preferred qualifications and responsibilities of the position), curriculum vitae, official transcripts, one or two recent scholarly publications or scholarly works in progress and three current letters of recommendation (within the past year) to: Special Education Faculty Search UAH College of Education Roberts Hall 323 301 Sparkman Drive Huntsville, AL 35899 kimberly.hile@uah.edu *Please note: digital submissions via e‐mail are preferred over mailed submissions. Please direct any questions related to the position to: Dr. Kimberly Hile, Associate Professor, Department of Curriculum and Instruction, College of Education, Sport and Human Sciences ( kimberly.hile@uah.edu ). Please refer to the log number 27-28-645 when applying."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#adjunct-position-in-the-department-of-kinesiology-part-time": {
      "title": "Adjunct Position in the Department of Kinesiology, Part-time",
      "description": "The University of Alabama in Huntsville invites applications for adjunct positions within the Department of Kinesiology. Specifically, positions are available within the Exercise Science, Sport and Fitness Management, and Physical Education Educator Preparation programs. These positions are part-time faculty positions. Persons hired in adjunct positions typically teach 3-6 hours of course work (1-2 classes) on an as-needed basis. Compensation is based on a standard university scale according to the number of credit hours being taught. Primary responsibilities include instruction based on the given student learning objective for the course, designing and implementing quality assessments, and grading students on their ability to master the learning objectives. Qualifications: Minimum requirements include a Master’s degree in Exercise Science, Sports Management, Physical Education, or a related field. Previous experience as an instructor is preferred. Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae, three professional references (including name, title, email address, and phone number), and official transcripts. If you are selected as a candidate for consideration by the department/committee, your references will be asked to submit a reference letter on your behalf, and you will be asked to provide official transcripts. For questions or concerns, please contact Rae Gill at rae.gill@uah.edu ."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#associate-dean-founding-executive-director": {
      "title": "ASSOCIATE DEAN & FOUNDING EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, UAH–HHHS NURSE ANESTHESIA ENTERPRISE",
      "description": "About the Position: The UAH College of Nursing seeks nominations and applications for the Associate Dean & Founding Executive Director of the UAH–HHHS Nurse Anesthesia Enterprise. This founding executive will lead the design, development, accreditation, launch and growth of UAH’s Nurse Anesthesia Program within a collaborative academic-clinical enterprise created by UAH and Huntsville Hospital Health System. UAH and HHHS have assembled the essential elements for launch: governing support, clinical access, four funded positions, a multi-year runway and the authority to build from first principles. The platform is ready. The founding executive will step in, bring the program to life and build it to national distinction. This appointment carries faculty rank (Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor) commensurate with qualifications and experience. Qualifications: A doctoral degree (DNP, DNAP, Ph.D. or equivalent). Current, unencumbered RN licensure and current CRNA certification, with eligibility for all required licensure and credentialing by the date of appointment. Current service as the director of an accredited nurse anesthesia program or demonstrated standing as a senior national program builder with a record of designing, developing, launching, strengthening or scaling significant nurse anesthesia programs. Multiple years of progressively responsible program-level leadership with material authority across curriculum, faculty, staff, clinical affiliations, budgets, operations, student progression and program outcomes. Direct experience leading accreditation preparation, review, reporting, compliance and continuous program evaluation under Council on Accreditation standards. A proven ability to build and scale enrollment, clinical capacity and operating infrastructure while sustaining academic quality, accreditation compliance and student outcomes. A record of recruiting, developing and retaining high-performing faculty and assembling the leadership team required to launch and grow a nurse anesthesia program. The executive judgment, credibility and communication skill to align university and health-system leaders, faculty, clinical partners and other stakeholders around a unified program strategy. Responsibilities: Provide executive leadership and accountability for the design, development, accreditation, launch, administration, growth and evaluation of the Nurse Anesthesia Program. Translate the shared UAH–HHHS commitment into one operating enterprise, aligning academic and clinical leadership, resources, decisions and execution around a unified program strategy. Build the program’s founding operating model, curriculum, faculty and staff, clinical affiliations, enrollment plan, budget and administrative systems. Lead accreditation preparation, review, reporting and continuous program evaluation in accordance with Council on Accreditation requirements and applicable university and clinical-affiliate policies. Recruit, lead and develop the high-performing faculty, staff and leadership team required to launch the program and scale it responsibly. Establish the standards, culture and performance disciplines required to build a nurse anesthesia program of national distinction. Teach and coordinate didactic, laboratory and clinical courses within the nurse anesthesia curriculum, using evidence-based instructional methods and the University’s learning management system. Advise and counsel students, and conduct the formative and summative evaluations required to document each student’s progression and achievement of program competencies. Provide oversight of doctoral scholarly projects and maintain clinical currency, CRNA certification and an active program of scholarship consistent with the faculty appointment. Contribute service to the College and University through committee participation, and engage in professional and community service that advances the program and the profession. About the College: The UAH College of Nursing is accredited by the Commission on Collegiate Nursing Education. The College offers undergraduate, master’s, post-graduate certificate and DNP programs, as well as a Joint Nursing Science Ph.D. program with The University of Alabama. Fall 2025 enrollment was just under 900 students, with plans in place to expand the number of nursing students rapidly in response to the growing demand across an expansive, economically vital region. The College offers extensive distance education and works with more than 1,300 clinical-education sites. Learn more on the college's website. About the University: The University of Alabama in Huntsville, part of The University of Alabama System, is a nationally acclaimed research university known for addressing significant technological challenges. UAH anchors Cummings Research Park, the second-largest research park in the United States and fourth-largest in the world. More than 300 enterprises and 26,000 engineering and technology professionals comprise an innovation ecosystem advancing aerospace, defense, biotechnology, cybersecurity, quantum computing, artificial intelligence, advanced engineering and the life sciences. Within this living laboratory, HudsonAlpha leads an advanced biotechnology corridor rivaling any in the United States; its genomic breakthroughs and translational research resonate worldwide. NASA’s Marshall Space Flight Center expands humanity’s reach beyond Earth. Redstone Arsenal serves as headquarters for U.S. Army Materiel Command, U.S. Army Space and Missile Defense Command and U.S. Space Command. Huntsville is home to the FBI’s expanding cybersecurity, intelligence and national-security operations. General Dynamics Electric Boat’s $2.4 billion submarine manufacturing complex connects the region’s talent directly to the Virginia-class fast-attack and Columbia-class ballistic-missile fleets. Here, research moves into application through quantum computing, space medicine, simulation and medical-device development. UAH includes six academic colleges, a Graduate School, an Honors College, more than 100 areas of study and 17 high-technology research centers. Learn more on the UAH website . About Huntsville Hospital Health System: Huntsville Hospital Health System is a market-defining, 15-hospital regional health system with approximately 3,000 beds, nearly 28,000 employees, more than 110,000 surgeries and more than 10,000 births annually. Its flagship hospital and core clinical operations are minutes from UAH, bringing together Level I trauma, cardiovascular and heart surgery, neurosurgery, orthopedics, advanced women’s and children’s services and high-volume community practice. This clinical depth gives the founding executive the scale, range and access required to design, launch and grow a consequential nurse anesthesia program within a major health system. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreation to a vibrant arts and entertainment scene, Huntsville offers a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  Livability named Huntsville the No. 1 Best Place to Live in the United States for 2026. More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: The position will remain open until filled, and applications will be reviewed as received. Nominations and confidential inquiries are encouraged. Candidates should submit a letter of interest and curriculum vitae to clare.perrin@uah.edu and addressed to: Haley M. Hoy, PhD, ACNP, FAANP, Chair c/o Clare Perrin Faculty Search Committee UAH College of Nursing Transcripts and references will be requested later in the search process. Please refer to log number 27-28-492 when applying."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#tenure-track-faculty-position-in-nursing": {
      "title": "TENURE-TRACK FACULTY POSITION IN NURSING, COLLEGE OF NURSING",
      "description": "About the Position: The full-time tenure-track position is a 9-month position teaching in the graduate program, including the Joint Nursing Science PhD and Doctor of Nursing Practice programs; however, candidates may be asked to teach across both undergraduate and graduate programs.  A PhD degree is required, preferably in nursing. A beginning program of research and scholarship befitting the rank of Assistant Professor is required. Faculty must engage in active teaching and learning strategies as well as demonstrate experience with online learning. Appointment Date: Fall 2026 About the College: The College of Nursing is accredited by the Commission on Collegiate Nursing Education (CCNE). The College offers pre-licensure BSN, accelerated BSN, ADN to BSN, ADN to MSN, MSN tracks in nursing administration, education, and nurse practitioner tracks in family and adult-gerontological acute care, post-graduate certificates, and DNP programs. The College of Nursing also offers a Joint Nursing Science PhD program with the University of Alabama. The College's total fall 2022 enrollment was just under 1,000 students across the degree programs. Extensive distance education programs are offered. The College of Nursing currently cooperates with more than 1300 sites for clinical education. All programs provide opportunities to make a difference in healthcare delivery, teaching, and evidence-based practice or research. More information about the college is available on its website. About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the city of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled.  Appointment date is anticipated as Fall 2026.  Interested candidates should submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, official transcripts, and three (3) letters of reference.  Digital submissions may be sent via e-mail to: facultysearch.con@uah.edu. Please direct any questions related to the position to: Amy Lanz, Ed.D., RN/Chair, Faculty Search Committee, UAH, College of Nursing, NB 417, 256-824-2453. Please refer to the log number 26-27-489 when applying."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#assistant-professor-in-the-field-of-heliophysics": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR IN THE FIELD OF HELIOPHYSICS, DEPARTMENT OF SPACE SCIENCE",
      "description": "About the Position: The Department of Space Science is seeking an outstanding scientist and teacher to fill a tenure-track assistant professor position in the field of heliophysics. This position will complement a National Science Foundation Established Program in Support of Competitive Research (EPSCoR) grant that was previously awarded to UAH. Within the general field of low temperature plasma physics, areas of particular interest to the Department include solar and interplanetary physics, especially research that supports the new Parker Solar Probe, Solar Orbiter, IMAP, and HelioSwarm missions in which the Department is engaged, particularly in the areas of coronal and solar wind physics including heating processes, turbulence, particle transport and energization (including pickup ion physics), and related space weather research. We are also looking to possibly expand departmental expertise in the area of magnetospheric and ionospheric physics and computational heliophysics, including AI/ML. The ideal candidate will complement existing strengths within the Department while expanding significantly research capabilities and competencies. We are seeking someone who uses theory, modeling, computation, and observations to address these areas of interest. The successful candidate will teach in accordance with a typical teaching load in the Department, will develop at least two new advanced graduate level courses reflecting their research expertise, and will support and mentor graduate students pursuing MSc and PhD degrees and undergraduate students in summer programs. Minimum Qualifications: The candidate must have a PhD or equivalent degree in Physics, Space Science, Astronomy & Astrophysics, or a demonstrably closely related field. The candidate must have completed a minimum of two years post-doctoral experience and must have published refereed international journal publications since graduating from their PhD or equivalent degree. The candidate must have a demonstrated record of having submitted proposals as a principal investigator to a national funding agency (either US or international). Preferred Qualifications : The candidate should have published at least one or more papers in international refereed journals such as The Astrophysical Journal, The Journal of Geophysical Research, Astronomy and Astrophysics, MNRAS, etc. annually since graduation with a PhD or equivalent, is or was a principal investigator on at least one federally (or nationally) funded research grant in the candidate’s field of expertise. Preference will be given to candidates who have evidence of excellence in teaching and experience in mentoring graduate students. Finally, active participation in appropriate professional organizations is preferred. Appointment Date: Beginning of Spring 2027 About the College: The College of Science at UAH is home to eight academic departments, including Atmospheric & Earth Science; Biological Sciences; Chemistry; Computer Science; Engineering Technology; Mathematical Sciences; Physics & Astronomy; and Space Science.  The College offers a range of rigorous, research-focused academic degree programs at the Bachelors, Masters, and PhD levels. We take pride in the innovative research our faculty and students perform, which is often conducted in collaboration with our community partners in Cummings Research Park, NASA Marshall Space Flight Center, and Redstone Arsenal. We strive to ensure low costs for students while maintaining high-quality standards, and by offering different course modalities, we serve the needs of various types of learners. The College actively promotes the professional development of our faculty and staff, encourages innovation in teaching, and seeks to create a welcoming and supportive environment that enables all of us to improve and realize our potential. We invite you to explore the UAH College of Science website for more information. About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Applicants must submit a cover letter, complete CV, a list of publications, a list of awarded research grants, a research statement, a teaching statement, and arrange to have three letters of reference sent to the SPA Space Physics Faculty Search Committee 2026, Department of Space Science, Cramer Research Hall, 320 Sparkman Drive, Huntsville, AL 35899 or by email to spa@uah.edu with the header “Faculty Space Physics Search Committee 2026” . Applications will remain open until the position is filled. Inquiries regarding the position or the application procedure can be directed to Prof. Gary Zank ( gpz0001@uah.edu ) and Dr. Keyvan Ghanbari ( kg0055@uah.edu ), respectively.  Please refer to log number 26-27-5008 when applying."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#eminent-scholar-and-professor-in-management-of-technology": {
      "title": "EMINENT SCHOLAR AND PROFESSOR IN MANAGEMENT OF TECHNOLOGY, DEPARTMENT OF INFORMATION SYSTEMS, SUPPLY CHAIN AND ANALYTICS",
      "description": "About the Position: The College of Business at The University of Alabama in Huntsville seeks a distinguished scholar and collaborative intellectual leader for the position of Eminent Scholar in Management of Technology. We are looking for an established research authority who is passionate about institutional impact and someone who is committed to mentoring early-career faculty, fostering collaborative research networks, and serving as a catalyst for departmental growth within a dynamic high-tech ecosystem. Responsibilities: Research & Intellectual Leadership: Maintain an active, nationally recognized research program targeting top-tier journals (e.g., ABDC A*, UTD 24, FT 50 lists) while actively fostering joint research initiatives and co-authorship opportunities within the department. Faculty Stewardship & Mentorship: Serve as a mentor and senior role model to junior faculty, guiding them through research trajectory development, grant pursuits, and navigation of the discipline. Teaching & Academic Excellence: Effectively teach and develop an up-to-date curriculum across undergraduate and graduate programs in information systems, cybersecurity management, supply chain management, or analytics. Actively support enrollment growth, student engagement, and student success in the department’s programs. Professional & Community Engagement: Participate in the profession, university service, and outreach to the local and global community. Connect the department’s research capabilities with Huntsville’s thriving defense, aerospace, and technology sector. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in information systems, supply chain management, business analytics, management science, or related business discipline is required. Academic track record qualifying for appointment at the rank of Professor. A distinguished, active publication record in top-tier business journals (e.g., ABDC A*, UTD 24, or FT 50 lists) demonstrating sustained scholarly leadership. Proven track record of collaborative research, including a history of co-authoring with and mentoring early-career researchers. Minimum of five years of demonstrated success teaching relevant subject areas in an AACSB-accredited business program. Preferred Qualifications: Ph.D. from an AACSB accredited institution. Demonstrated success in building inter- and intra-departmental research collaborations and industry-academic partnerships. Experience creating, enhancing, or leading academic programs in IS, analytics, cybersecurity, or supply chain management. Track record or interest in securing external research funding and engaging with business, defense, and federal agencies and organizations. Appointment Date: As early as Fall 2027 About the College: The College of Business is accredited by AACSB International and has approximately 35 tenured, tenure-track, and clinical faculty, 30 part-time faculty, 1,050 undergraduate students, and 350 graduate students. The college offers a BSBA degree with majors in accounting, analytics, finance, information systems, management, and marketing; a BS degree in economics and computational analysis; and six graduate degrees: an MBA, and specialized master’s degrees in business analytics, cybersecurity, human resource management, information systems, as well as supply chain management. UAH has been ranked by the NSF in recent years among the top 10 in the nation for federal funding of research in management and economics. The college hosts two centers and one office that provide outreach, service, and engagement with the greater community. The Center for Management and Economics Research, Small Business Development Center, and Apex Accelerator, and Office of Professional and Continuing Education serve individuals, businesses, and organizations in the community and throughout the U.S. with business consulting and non-degree educational services. The College of Business also values its collaborative relationship with the UAH College of Engineering, especially in the Engineering Management program in the Industrial & Systems Engineering and Engineering Management Department. For detailed information about the college and its programs, please visit the UAH College of Business webpage . About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Applicants must submit the following via email to the search committee at esmotsearch@uah.edu : a letter of interest reflecting the alignment of their background for the position, including their research agenda, teaching philosophy, and approach to mentorship, collaboration, and collegial engagement, a curriculum vita, evidence of teaching effectiveness, a representative sample of recent research work, and identification of three references. Up-to-date letters of recommendation and official transcripts of academic work will be required for finalists. It is preferred that applications be submitted by December 1, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted until the position is filled. Please refer to log number: 26-27-141 when applying."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#clinical-assistant-professor": {
      "title": "CLINICAL ASSISTANT PROFESSOR, DEPARTMENT OF BIOLOGICAL SCIENCES",
      "description": "About the Position: The Department of Biological Sciences at The University of Alabama in Huntsville (UAH) invites applications for a Clinical Assistant Professor with an anticipated Spring 2027 start date. This is an instructional faculty position with primary responsibilities supporting the department’s lower-division biology laboratory and lecture curriculum. The successful candidate will provide instructional leadership and coordination for the department’s introductory biology laboratories. Primary responsibilities will include: Supervising teaching assistants and coordinating all sections of BYS 121 Principles of Biology Laboratory and BYS 122 Organismal Biology Laboratory; Updating and maintaining course curricula, laboratory materials, and syllabi for BYS 121 and BYS 122; Teaching one section of BYS 119 Principles of Biology or BYS 120 Organismal Biology; and Serving as laboratory manager for the lower-division teaching laboratories in the Department of Biological Sciences. Instructional responsibilities are the primary focus of this position. Opportunities may also exist for involvement in undergraduate research, biology education research, or collaborative projects with faculty in the Department of Biological Sciences. Minimum Qualifications: Applicants must possess a Ph.D. in Biological Sciences or a closely related field by the appointment date and must demonstrate academic preparation appropriate for teaching introductory college-level biology. Preferred Qualifications: The ideal candidate will demonstrate: Experience teaching undergraduate biology lecture and/or laboratory courses; Experience coordinating multi-section laboratory courses; Experience supervising or mentoring graduate teaching assistants, undergraduate teaching assistants, or other instructional personnel; Experience developing or updating laboratory curriculum, instructional materials, and course syllabi; Experience with the organization, operation, or management of undergraduate teaching laboratories; Evidence of effective teaching and a commitment to undergraduate student success; and Interest or experience in undergraduate research, biology education research, or collaborative scholarly activities. Appointment Date: Spring 2027 About the College: The College of Science at UAH is home to eight academic departments, including Atmospheric & Earth Science; Biological Sciences; Chemistry; Computer Science; Engineering Technology; Mathematical Sciences; Physics & Astronomy; and Space Science. The College offers a range of rigorous, research-focused academic degree programs at the Bachelors, Masters, and PhD levels. We take pride in the innovative research our faculty and students perform, which is often conducted in collaboration with our community partners in Cummings Research Park, NASA Marshall Space Flight Center, and Redstone Arsenal. We strive to ensure low costs for students while maintaining high-quality standards, and by offering different course modalities, we serve the needs of various types of learners. The College actively promotes the professional development of our faculty and staff, encourages innovation in teaching, and seeks to create a welcoming and supportive environment that enables all of us to improve and realize our potential. We invite you to explore the UAH College of Science website for more information. About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the city of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Applicants should submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, statement of teaching philosophy, and contact information for three professional references electronically to clinical_biology_search@uah.edu . Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. The anticipated start date for the position is January 2027. Please refer to log number 27-28-5010 when applying."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#eminent-scholar-chair-in-the-humanities-visiting-professor": {
      "title": "EMINENT SCHOLAR CHAIR IN THE HUMANITIES (VISITING PROFESSOR), THE HUMANITIES CENTER AT THE UNIVERSITY OF ALABAMA IN HUNTSVILLE",
      "description": "About the Position: The University of Alabama in Huntsville (UAH) invites applications for Visiting Professor of Modern East Asian Studies with a preference for Modern Chinese History. Possible secondary interests in intelligence and security studies, public history, or digital humanities are also welcome. This is a 9-month appointment lasting from August 2027 to May 2028. The successful applicant will be appointed as Eminent Scholar Chair in the Humanities and be eligible for certain university benefits. The Eminent Scholar Chair in the Humanities enjoys the use of a private faculty office, access to library and other faculty resources, as well as access to funding for expenses directly related to their scholarly work. Appointments are also subject to approval by the Chancellor and Board of Trustees for the University of Alabama System. Responsibilities : The successful applicant will be expected to offer one in-person course per semester (Fall and Spring) related to their specialization, give at least two public lectures on a topic relating to their research, lead at least one meeting with faculty regarding curriculum development or research, and be prepared to mentor junior faculty and graduate students in the humanities. Required Qualifications : terminal degree in a humanities discipline current or past appointment as Full Professor at an accredited institution either national or international recognition of past and original contributions to a humanities discipline or national or international recognition as a public intellectual Preferred Qualifications: demonstrated record of excellence in teaching experience mentoring junior faculty and graduate students successful track record of interdisciplinary collaboration Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H-1B petition. Appointment Date: August 2027 About the Humanities Center: The Humanities Center at The University of Alabama in Huntsville coordinates opportunities for members of the UAH campus and the Huntsville region to experience and benefit from the skills and knowledge that derive from the humanities. The Center supports faculty from a range of disciplines, including Art, Art History, and Design, Communication Arts, English, History, Music, Philosophy, Political Science, Sociology, Theatre & Film, and World Languages & Cultures. The Center provides faculty with a wide range of funding opportunities. These include: research grants for humanities-related projects; travel grants to support attendance at professional conferences relating to the humanities; library enhancement grants for faculty-curated additions to the collections at the M. Louis Salmon Library; public programming grants to enrich the intellectual and professional lives of UAH faculty and students as well as the Huntsville community; and book manuscript workshop grants to help authors strengthen book proposals, connect with experts in their areas of specialization, and raise the profile of humanities and humanities faculty within the academic community. For more information, please visit the UAH Humanities Center webpage . About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Applicants should submit the following materials via email to the Center’s staff assistant, Ms. Tina Stallings, at trs0040@uah.edu : (1) letter of application addressing position qualifications and responsibilities (no longer than three pages) (2) curriculum vitae (3) writing sample (3) academic transcripts (4) three letters of recommendation Application materials should be submitted and received by the Center by January 4, 2027 .  The receipt of applications does not guarantee that this position will be filled, and The University of Alabama in Huntsville reserves the right to withdraw this invitation for applications at any time and for any reason.  Please refer to log number 27-28-416 when applying."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#assistant-professor-of-music-director-of-bands": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR OF MUSIC, DIRECTOR OF BANDS, DEPARTMENT OF MUSIC, THEATRE, AND FILM",
      "description": "About the Position: The Department of Music, Theatre, and Film at The University of Alabama in Huntsville (UAH) invites applications for an Assistant Professor (tenure-track) of Music, Director of Bands position. We seek applicants who welcome the challenges and rewards of serving on a diverse and artistically talented faculty of a growing department, located in a dynamic and thriving arts community. Responsibilities: Provide effective leadership as the conductor of the UAH Wind Ensemble, coupled with a recruiting component integral to the growth of the department. Additional responsibilities include instruction in the areas of conducting and music education, and overseeing the leadership of the university athletic bands and instrumental area. The regular teaching load is three courses per semester. Faculty are expected to actively engage as leaders and collaborators in scholarly and creative endeavors within their research professions and the local community. Qualifications: D.M./D.M.A. in Wind Band Conducting and an ability to effectively rehearse/conduct wind ensemble/band, as well as highly effective instruction in undergraduate/graduate courses related to conducting and music education. Ideal candidates should have experience in college teaching and a proven track record of successful scholarship related to wind conducting, effective rehearsal technique, and collegiate ensemble performances showcased in concerts, conferences, and festivals. Additional experience in orchestral conducting a plus. ABDs will be considered; however, all requirements for the doctoral degree must be completed by August 1, 2027, to be appointed at the rank of Assistant Professor. Appointment Date: August 4, 2027 About the College: The College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences at The University of Alabama in Huntsville has nine departments, supporting fifteen bachelor’s degree programs, five master’s programs, one Ph.D. program, and eight certificate programs. The college also has a number of interdisciplinary minors, including Global Studies; Women’s, Gender, & Sexuality Studies; Justice and Equity Studies; and Game Design and Development. Our class sizes are relatively small, and our faculty are committed to students’ success. Our college is housed across three separate buildings on campus, including the newly refurbished Morton Hall, with state-of-the-art facilities for teaching, research and creative activity. Applicants may find more information about the College, the University, and Huntsville at https://www.uah.edu/ahs/prospective-faculty . About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applicants are encouraged to submit their materials promptly for full consideration. The following materials should be submitted in PDF format via email to the department's senior staff assistant, Michele Pullum, at michele.pullum@uah.edu . 1) Cover letter explaining your interest and suitability for the position 2) Curriculum vitae 3) Statement of teaching philosophy 4) Statement of creative activity and scholarly work 5) Links to 15-20 minutes of video showcasing the candidate conducting a concert band in rehearsal and in performance 6) List of three references with contact info (finalists will be asked for letters of recommendation) Please refer to log number 27-28-417 when applying ."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#tenure-track-assistant-professor-in-entertainment-technology": {
      "title": "TENURE-TRACK, ASSISTANT PROFESSOR IN ENTERTAINMENT TECHNOLOGY, DEPARTMENT OF MUSIC, THEATRE, AND FILM",
      "description": "About the Position: The Department of Music, Theatre, and Film at The University of Alabama in Huntsville (UAH) invites applications for a dynamic and visionary educator and practitioner of Film and Media Arts, who will teach, mentor, and inspire students preparing for careers in creative entertainment industries. The successful candidate will bring expertise in film production and directing, and a contemporary understanding of the changing aspects of the production process and the field, including the emergence of AI usage in film production. Responsibilities: Engage in creative activity/research, teaching and service as part of the Theatre Program, which offers majors in Theatre with concentrations in Film and Media Arts, Design and Technology, and Performance. Teaching responsibilities include instruction in the areas of video production, film producing, and film studies. The regular teaching load is three courses per semester. Faculty are expected to actively engage in recruiting events, in public presentations of student work, and to meaningfully contribute to departmental productions. Qualifications: M.F.A/M.A./Ph.D. in Film and Video Production, Film Studies/Communication, or a related field and an ability to effectively develop, produce, shoot, and complete video and film projects, as well as highly effective instruction in undergraduate courses related to film and media arts. Ideal candidates should have experience in college teaching related to film and video production, film studies, and media arts. Additional experience in the contemporary producing and directing business of film is a plus. Candidates who are near completing their degree requirements will be considered; however, all degree requirements must be completed by August 1, 2027, to be appointed at the rank of Assistant Professor. Appointment Date: August 2, 2027 About the College: The College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences at The University of Alabama in Huntsville has nine departments, supporting fifteen bachelor’s degree programs, five master’s programs, one Ph.D. program, and eight certificate programs.  The college also has a number of interdisciplinary minors, including Global Studies; Women’s, Gender, & Sexuality Studies; Justice and Equity Studies; and Game Design and Development. Our class sizes are relatively small, and our faculty are committed to students’ success.  Our college is housed across three separate buildings on campus, including the newly refurbished Morton Hall, with state-of-the-art facilities for teaching, research and creative activity.  Applicants may find more information about the College, the University, and Huntsville at https://www.uah.edu/ahs/prospective-faculty . About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Review of applications will begin January 12, 2027, and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit the following materials in PDF format, via email, to the department's senior staff assistant, Michele Pullum at michele.pullum@uah.edu . 1) Cover letter explaining your interest and suitability for the position 2) Curriculum vitae 3) Statement of teaching philosophy 4) Statement of creative activity and scholarly work 5) Links to 3 completed film projects 6) List of three references with contact info (finalists will be asked for letters of recommendation) Please refer to the log number 27-28-418 when applying."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#assistant-professor-in-ancient-and-medieval-studies": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR IN ANCIENT AND MEDIEVAL STUDIES, DEPARTMENT OF HISTORY",
      "description": "About the Position: The Department of History at The University of Alabama in Huntsville is accepting applications for a tenure-track position in Ancient and Medieval Studies . Preference is for candidates with a specialization in Roman history and the ability to teach archaeology or digital humanities. This candidate will support the Ancient and Medieval Studies (AMS) interdisciplinary program and may serve as the AMS program coordinator. The teaching load is three courses (nine credit hours) per semester and may include online, hybrid, and traditional in person formats. The candidate will teach the AMS program’s entry course, Ancient and Medieval Worlds, along with survey courses in world history and upper-level and graduate-level courses in Roman or related history. Ideally, the candidate will also teach courses in archaeology or digital humanities. The successful candidate will have an active research agenda and demonstrate evidence of effective teaching or the potential for effective teaching. Service to the institution, including participation in College and University-level committees, is expected. Qualifications :  Ph.D. in History required. Ph.D. should be in hand by the start date. A strong commitment to teaching is required. Appointment Date: August 2027 About the College: The College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences at The University of Alabama in Huntsville (UAH) has nine departments, supporting sixteen bachelor’s degree programs, three master’s programs, one Ph.D. program, and five certificate programs at the undergraduate and graduate level. Our college is housed across three separate buildings on campus, including the newly refurbished Morton Hall, with state-of-the-art facilities for teaching, research and creative activity. Our class sizes are relatively small, and our faculty are committed to students’ success. Applicants may find more information about the College, the University, and Huntsville on the College’s webpage . About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Candidates should submit a cover letter that discusses their research activity and plans, C.V., three letters of reference, a 1-page statement describing their teaching approaches and methods, and graduate transcripts. Send applications to Christine Sears, Chair, Department of History, The University of Alabama in Huntsville, Huntsville, AL 35899, at history@uah.edu . Please reference log number 27-28-420 when applying. The first date of consideration is November 12, 2026. All applications received by November 12th will be given full consideration; those submitted later will be considered until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#assistant-professor-of-technical-and-professional-communicat": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR OF TECHNICAL AND PROFESSIONAL COMMUNICATION, DEPARTMENT OF ENGLISH",
      "description": "About the Position: The Department of English at The University of Alabama in Huntsville invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Technical and Professional Communication. The successful candidate will hold a Ph.D. in English with an emphasis in technical and professional communication, or in a related field. Candidates should demonstrate expertise in technical and professional communication and maintain a research agenda engaging artificial intelligence, large language models, and their implications for writing and communication. The successful candidate will contribute to the department's undergraduate and graduate programs, including the Technical and Professional Communication concentration in the English B.A., the M.A. in English, and graduate certificates in Technical Communication and User Experience. Teaching responsibilities may include technical and professional writing, technical editing, user experience, writing with large language models, rhetoric and artificial intelligence, and related courses. The position offers opportunities for curriculum development, graduate student mentorship, and interdisciplinary collaboration, particularly in areas involving emerging technologies, workplace communication, and the rhetorical, ethical, and practical implications of artificial intelligence. The regular teaching load is three courses (9 credit hours) per semester and may include undergraduate and graduate courses in traditional and online formats. The successful candidate will also be expected to maintain an active research agenda and contribute service to the department, college, university, and profession. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in English with an emphasis in technical and professional communication, or in a related field. Demonstrated expertise in technical and professional communication. Evidence of successful teaching or demonstrated potential for effective teaching in technical and professional communication at the university level. An active research agenda engaging artificial intelligence, large language models, or related emerging technologies in writing and communication. Preferred Qualifications Record of scholarly publication or demonstrated potential for significant scholarship in technical and professional communication, particularly involving artificial intelligence and large language models. Experience teaching or developing courses addressing artificial intelligence, large language models, or emerging technologies in writing and communication. Expertise in user experience, usability, information design, technical editing, or related areas of technical and professional communication. Demonstrated experience developing and delivering effective online undergraduate or graduate instruction is strongly preferred. Ability to contribute to interdisciplinary programs, including Interdisciplinary Studies and User Experience, through research-methods instruction, interdisciplinary teaching, student mentorship, or capstone supervision is strongly preferred. Ability to contribute to graduate instruction, student mentorship, and curriculum development. Appointment Date:  Fall 2027 About the Department: The Department of English at The University of Alabama in Huntsville offers undergraduate and graduate programs in literature, Creative Writing, and Technical and Professional Communication. The department's redesigned B.A. in English includes concentrations in these three areas, complemented by minors in literature, Creative Writing, and Technical Writing. The department also offers an M.A. in English and graduate certificates in Technical Communication and User Experience. In addition to serving English majors and minors, the department provides First-Year Writing and general education literature courses for students across the university. Faculty contribute to interdisciplinary teaching and scholarship and support the university's commitment to student success through undergraduate and graduate instruction, mentorship, and community engagement. More information is available on the Department of English webpage. About the College: The College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences at The University of Alabama in Huntsville has nine departments, supporting sixteen bachelor’s degree programs, three master’s programs, one Ph.D. program, and five certificate programs at the undergraduate and graduate level. Our college is housed across three separate buildings on campus, including the newly refurbished Morton Hall, with state-of-the-art facilities for teaching, research and creative activity. Our class sizes are relatively small, and our faculty are committed to students’ success. Applicants may find more information about the College, the University, and Huntsville on the College’s webpage . About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue through December 1, 2026. Applicants should submit a letter of interest (describing your qualifications for the position, including relevant research and teaching experience), a writing sample, a teaching philosophy, and a curriculum vitae. Submit your application to Dr. Chad Thomas at eh@uah.edu. Please include the words “TPC Application” in the subject line. Please direct any questions related to the position to Dr. Chad Thomas at cat0009@uah.edu . Also, please reference log number 27-28-419 when applying."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#full-time-lecturer-in-english": {
      "title": "FULL-TIME LECTURER IN ENGLISH, DEPARTMENT OF ENGLISH",
      "description": "About the Position :  The Department of English at The University of Alabama in Huntsville invites applications for a full-time Lecturer in English beginning Fall 2027. The successful candidate will hold an M.A., M.F.A., or Ph.D. in English, Creative Writing, or a closely related field and demonstrate a commitment to effective undergraduate teaching. The successful candidate will primarily teach First-Year Writing courses and 200-level general education literature surveys. Literature assignments may include broad surveys and courses organized around particular themes, genres, or cultural traditions. Teaching responsibilities will also typically include one Creative Writing course per academic year. Additional assignments may occasionally include upper-division literature courses, depending on departmental needs and the candidate's expertise. The regular teaching load is four courses (12 credit hours) per semester and may include both traditional and online formats. The position requires a commitment to student success, effective undergraduate teaching, and participation in departmental service. The successful candidate may also have opportunities to contribute to interdisciplinary programs. Required Qualifications Earned M.A., M.F.A., or Ph.D. in English, Creative Writing, or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Demonstrated experience teaching First-Year Writing at the college level. Demonstrated ability to teach 200-level general education literature surveys, supported by experience teaching literature at the college level. Evidence of effective teaching. Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching Creative Writing courses. Experience teaching upper-division literature courses. Demonstrated experience developing and delivering effective online instruction at the college level is strongly preferred. Ability to contribute to interdisciplinary programs, such as Interdisciplinary Studies. Appointment Date: Fall 2027 About the Department: The Department of English at The University of Alabama in Huntsville offers undergraduate and graduate programs in literature, Creative Writing, and Technical and Professional Communication. The department's redesigned B.A. in English includes concentrations in these three areas, complemented by minors in literature, Creative Writing, and Technical Writing. The department also offers an M.A. in English and graduate certificates in Technical Communication and User Experience. In addition to serving English majors and minors, the department provides First-Year Writing and general education literature courses for students across the university. Faculty contribute to interdisciplinary teaching and scholarship and support the university's commitment to student success through undergraduate and graduate instruction, mentorship, and community engagement. Applicants can find more information on the Department of English webpage. About the College: The College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences at The University of Alabama in Huntsville has nine departments, supporting sixteen bachelor’s degree programs, three master’s programs, one Ph.D. program, and five certificate programs at the undergraduate and graduate level. Our college is housed across three separate buildings on campus, including the newly refurbished Morton Hall, with state-of-the-art facilities for teaching, research and creative activity. Our class sizes are relatively small, and our faculty are committed to students’ success. Applicants may find more information about the College, the University, and Huntsville on the College’s webpage . About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue through January 22, 2027. Applicants should submit a letter of interest (describing their qualifications for the position, including relevant teaching experience), a teaching philosophy, and a curriculum vitae. Submit your application to Dr. Chad Thomas at eh@uah.edu. Please include the words “Lecturer Application” in the subject line. Please direct any questions related to the position to Dr. Chad Thomas at cat0009@uah.edu . Please reference log number 27-28-422 when applying."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#assistant-professor-in-computer-engineering": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR IN COMPUTER ENGINEERING, DEPARTMENT OF ELECTRICAL AND COMPUTER ENGINEERING",
      "description": "About the Position: The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at The University of Alabama in Huntsville (UAH) invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Computer Engineering/Cybersecurity Engineering. Applications are welcome in all areas of computer and cybersecurity engineering, with priority given to candidates conducting research in computer systems; cybersecurity; applied AI/ML, including secure and trustworthy agentic AI systems; AI at the edge; and related areas. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in computer engineering, cybersecurity engineering, electrical engineering, computer science, or a closely related field by the appointment date. The successful candidate will demonstrate the potential to establish a nationally recognized, externally funded research program; a commitment to effective teaching and student mentoring; and a willingness to contribute to university and professional service. Appointment Date: August 2027 About the Department: The Department offers Bachelor of Science (B.S.) degrees in electrical, computer, and cybersecurity engineering; Master of Science (M.S.) and Doctor of Philosophy (Ph.D.) degrees in electrical and computer engineering; and interdisciplinary M.S. degrees in software engineering and cybersecurity. Our faculty currently includes 20 tenured or tenure-track members and three full-time lecturers. Our student body includes approximately 660 undergraduate and 160 graduate students. Our faculty’s research interests include electronic devices, applied electromagnetics and optics, signal and image processing, controls, the Internet of Things (IoT), computer systems, cybersecurity, software engineering, and quantum systems. The Department is engaged in a wide range of research activities funded by NSF, DoD, NASA, DOE, NSA, Redstone Arsenal, the State of Alabama, and several corporate sponsors. About the College: The College of Engineering comprises five departments and has the largest enrollment among the University’s academic colleges. The College’s research addresses three of the National Academy of Engineering (NAE) Grand Challenges for Engineering: securing cyberspace, restoring and improving urban infrastructure, and engineering the tools of scientific discovery. Our strategic Flight Plan 2032 seeks to enhance student-centered teaching and learning focused on student success, promote basic and applied research, and engage the local and regional community through outreach and workforce development. About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Application materials must be submitted in a single PDF file and include a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, contact information for at least three references (including names, addresses, email addresses, and telephone numbers), and separate statements of research plans and teaching interests. Application materials should be sent via email to ecesearch@uah.edu . Please refer to 27-28-290 when applying."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#assistant-professor-in-electrical-engineering": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR IN ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING, DEPARTMENT OF ELECTRICAL AND COMPUTER ENGINEERING",
      "description": "About the Position: The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at The University of Alabama in Huntsville (UAH) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Electrical Engineering. Applications are welcome in all areas of electrical engineering, with priority given to candidates conducting research in radar; signal processing, including AI-enabled methods; high-power microwave systems; robotics and autonomous systems; optics; and related areas. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in electrical engineering, computer engineering, or a closely related field by the appointment date. The successful candidate will demonstrate the potential to establish a nationally recognized, externally funded research program; a commitment to effective teaching and student mentoring; and a willingness to contribute to university and professional service. Appointment Date: August 2027 About the Department: The Department offers Bachelor of Science (B.S.) degrees in electrical, computer, and cybersecurity engineering; Master of Science (M.S.) and Doctor of Philosophy (Ph.D.) degrees in electrical and computer engineering; and interdisciplinary M.S. degrees in software engineering and cybersecurity. Our faculty currently includes 20 tenured or tenure-track members and three full-time lecturers. Our student body includes approximately 660 undergraduate and 160 graduate students. Our faculty’s research interests include electronic devices, applied electromagnetics and optics, signal and image processing, controls, the Internet of Things (IoT), computer systems, cybersecurity, software engineering, and quantum systems. The Department is engaged in a wide range of research activities funded by NSF, DoD, NASA, DOE, NSA, Redstone Arsenal, the State of Alabama, and several corporate sponsors. About the College: The College of Engineering comprises five departments and has the largest enrollment among the University’s academic colleges. The College’s research addresses three of the National Academy of Engineering (NAE) Grand Challenges for Engineering: securing cyberspace, restoring and improving urban infrastructure, and engineering the tools of scientific discovery. Our strategic Flight Plan 2032 seeks to enhance student-centered teaching and learning focused on student success, promote basic and applied research, and engage the local and regional community through outreach and workforce development. About the University :  Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Application materials must be submitted in a single PDF and include a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, contact information for at least three references (including names, addresses, email addresses, and telephone numbers), and separate statements of research plans and teaching interests. Application materials should be sent via email to ecesearch@uah.edu . Please reference 27-28-287 when applying. Consideration of applications will begin immediately, and the search will remain open until the position is filled. Preference will be given to applications received before December 1, 2026. Attendance at new-faculty orientation sessions is required."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#assistant-professor-specializing-in-science-fiction-and-spec": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR SPECIALIZING IN SCIENCE FICTION AND SPECULATIVE LITERATURE, DEPARTMENT OF ENGLISH",
      "description": "About the Position: The Department of English at The University of Alabama in Huntsville invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor specializing in Science Fiction and Speculative Literature. The successful candidate will hold a Ph.D. in English, Literary Studies, or a related discipline by the time of appointment. Candidates should demonstrate expertise in science fiction and/or speculative literature studies. Special consideration will be given to those with complementary expertise in African American literature, literatures of the American South, and/or Critical Theory. The successful candidate will contribute to the department's undergraduate and graduate programs with a preference for those capable of developing classes for both traditional and online modes of instruction. Teaching responsibilities will include themed sophomore literature surveys like “Speculative Realities,” upper-division courses in science fiction and speculative literature, and graduate seminars structured around chosen topics that expand the department’s range of class offerings for M.A. students. Depending on the candidate’s expertise, additional teaching responsibilities may include EH 403/503 Literary Criticism and Theory and EH 615 Critical Theory. Candidates must also be willing to direct and serve as an additional reader on graduate theses and capstone projects. The successful candidate will also contribute to teaching methods and research courses within a growing Interdisciplinary Studies program. The regular teaching load is three courses (9 credit hours) per semester. The successful candidate will also be expected to maintain an active research agenda and contribute service to the department, college, university, and profession. In addition to regular support by university and college funds, UAH's Humanities Center provides additional opportunities for research support, conference travel, and public humanities programming initiatives. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in English, Literary Studies, or a related discipline, with a research focus in science fiction and/or speculative literature. Demonstrated expertise in science fiction and/or speculative literature. Evidence of successful teaching or demonstrated potential for effective teaching in science fiction, speculative literature, and other literature classes at the university level. An active research agenda engaging with some area of science fiction and/or speculative literature studies. Willingness and ability to teach research methods and capstone courses in the Interdisciplinary Studies program. Preferred Qualifications: Record of scholarly publication or demonstrated potential for significant scholarship in the field of science fiction and/or speculative literature studies. Secondary expertise in African American Studies, New Southern Studies, and/or literary and cultural theory. Evidence of successful teaching or demonstrated potential for effective teaching of African American literature, literatures of the U.S. South, and/or literary and cultural theory. Demonstrated experience developing and delivering effective online undergraduate or graduate instruction is strongly preferred. Ability to contribute to graduate instruction, student mentorship, and curriculum development. Priority will be given to candidates whose research and teaching complement or enhance existing strengths in undergraduate research mentorship and interdisciplinary projects. Appointment Date:  Fall 2027 About the Department: The Department of English at The University of Alabama in Huntsville offers undergraduate and graduate programs in literature, Creative Writing, and Technical and Professional Communication. The department's redesigned B.A. in English includes concentrations in these three areas, complemented by minors in literature, Creative Writing, and Technical Writing. The department also offers an M.A. in English and graduate certificates in Technical Communication and User Experience. In addition to serving English majors and minors, the department provides First-Year Writing and general education literature courses for students across the university. Faculty contribute to interdisciplinary teaching and scholarship and support the university's commitment to student success through undergraduate and graduate instruction, mentorship, and community engagement. More information is available on the Department of English webpage. About the College: The College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences at The University of Alabama in Huntsville has nine departments, supporting sixteen bachelor’s degree programs, three master’s programs, one Ph.D. program, and five certificate programs at the undergraduate and graduate level. Our college is housed across three separate buildings on campus, including the newly refurbished Morton Hall, with state-of-the-art facilities for teaching, research and creative activity. Our class sizes are relatively small, and our faculty are committed to students’ success. Applicants may find more information about the College, the University, and Huntsville on the College’s webpage . About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Review of applications will begin immediately and continue through December 1, 2026. Applicants should submit a letter of interest (describing their qualifications for the position, including relevant research and teaching experience), a writing sample, a teaching philosophy, and a curriculum vitae. Submit applications to Dr. Chad Thomas at eh@uah.edu. Please include the words “SF Application” in the subject line. Please direct any questions related to the position to Dr. Chad Thomas at cat0009@uah.edu . Also, reference log number 27-28-423 on your application."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#lecturer-in-clinical-or-counseling-psychology": {
      "title": "LECTURER IN CLINICAL OR COUNSELING PSYCHOLOGY, DEPARTMENT OF PSYCHOLOGY",
      "description": "About the Position: The Department of Psychology at The University of Alabama in Huntsville invites applications for a Lecturer in Clinical or Counseling Psychology. We seek a passionate educator in one of these areas to support our undergraduate and graduate programs. Candidates with teaching experience and excellence in teaching will be given priority. The successful candidate will lead the undergraduate Psychological Services concentration within the major by teaching required courses, managing the internship program, and advising student organizations. Lecturers whose teaching complements or enhances existing strengths are especially encouraged to apply. Primary responsibilities:  The successful candidate is expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses such as General Psychology, Lifespan Development, Personality, Developmental Psychology, Introduction to Clinical and Counseling Psychology, Theories of Counseling, Internship, Assessment, Substance Abuse Therapy, Trauma Focused Therapy, The Helping Relationship, and Crisis Intervention. The teaching load is 4 courses per semester and may include online and traditional format courses. Minimum qualifications: Ph.D. or Psy.D. in clinical or counseling psychology. Evidence of demonstrated or potential excellence in undergraduate and/or graduate instruction. Evidence of demonstrated or potential excellence in mentoring student internship courses and/or working with student organizations. Preferred qualifications: Specialization in clinical or counseling psychology with experience in mentoring students. Experience with placing and/or supervising trainees in clinical or counseling settings. Experience developing graduate and undergraduate curriculum and programs. Ability to collaborate with other units and colleagues within the university and the community. Experience in developing, implementing, and building sustainable collaborative relationships with local mental health facilities, counseling centers, and related organizations. Additional Information:  The Department of Psychology offers two undergraduate degrees, a B.A. with a concentration in psychological services and a B.S. with a concentration in psychological sciences, as well as a 4+1 joint undergraduate and Master’s degree (JUMP). In addition, the department offers the M.S. in Experimental Psychology with an optional concentration in Industrial/ Organizational Psychology, and a Ph.D. in Applied Experimental Psychology with concentrations in Human Factors and Applied Experimental Psychology (Ph.D. students minor in quantitative psychology). The department is also preparing to offer a M.A. in Clinical Mental Health Counseling. Psychology department faculty focus on direct mentoring of undergraduate and graduate students. Faculty teach courses in many areas of psychology, including clinical, experimental, social, developmental, cognitive, biological, perceptual, and industrial/organizational, in addition to courses cross-listed with engineering. Rank and Salary:  This is a non-tenure track, Lecturer Rank position. Salary will be competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. An excellent and comprehensive benefits package is available. Appointment Date:  Anticipated start date is Fall 2027 About the College: The College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences at The University of Alabama in Huntsville has nine departments, supporting sixteen bachelor’s degree programs, three master’s programs, one Ph.D. program, and five certificate programs at the undergraduate and graduate level. Our college is housed across three separate buildings on campus, including the newly refurbished Morton Hall, with state-of-the-art facilities for teaching, research and creative activity. Our class sizes are relatively small, and our faculty are committed to students’ success. Applicants may find more information about the College, the University, and Huntsville on the College’s website . About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline :  Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Interested candidates should submit a letter of interest (relating your experiences to the required/preferred qualifications and responsibilities of the position), curriculum vitae, unofficial transcripts, teaching philosophy, and evidence of teaching effectiveness. Three letters of recommendation will be requested for finalists. Please send application materials to Beth Hodges, Staff Assistant, Departments of Psychology and Sociology, at ejh0028@uah.edu. Please refer to log number: 27-28-424 when applying. Review of applications will begin on November 30, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please direct any questions related to the position to Dr. Dan Morrison, Interim Chair, Department of Psychology, drm0047@uah.edu (Chair of the Selection Committee), or Beth Hodges, ejh0028@uah.edu."
    },
    "https://www.uah.edu/hr/careers/faculty-careers#assistant-professor-in-counseling-psychology": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR IN COUNSELING PSYCHOLOGY, DEPARTMENT OF PSYCHOLOGY",
      "description": "About the Position: The University of Alabama in Huntsville invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in Counseling Psychology. The ideal candidate will hold a Ph.D. in Counseling Psychology and have an active research agenda. Research areas within Counseling Psychology are open; preference for candidates whose research complements new courses for the proposed M.A. in Clinical Mental Health Counseling. The department’s strengths include industrial/organizational psychology, human factors, cognition, learning, and memory. We especially encourage applications from individuals with strong teaching skills and/or demonstrated professional development related to teaching at the undergraduate and/or graduate levels. The successful candidate will teach two courses per semester at the undergraduate and/or graduate level in areas such as Personality, Adjustment and Adaptation, Developmental, Introduction to Counseling Psychology, Assessment, Substance Abuse Therapy, Trauma Focused Therapy, The Helping Relationship, Crisis Intervention, and course(s) in the candidate’s area of expertise. In addition, the faculty member will supervise undergraduate and graduate students conducting research in their area of specialty. Required Qualifications: Earned Ph.D. in Counseling Psychology or a closely related field from an accredited institution. Demonstrated evidence of scholarly productivity or the potential for scholarly productivity (e.g., peer-reviewed manuscripts, presentations, external funding). Demonstrated evidence of ability to pursue a defined research agenda in Psychology. Demonstrated evidence of successfully mentoring Master’s and/or Ph.D. students in Psychology, or the potential for successful mentoring. Preferred Qualifications: Experience developing graduate and undergraduate curriculum and programs. Experience teaching university students in online, hybrid, and/or face-to-face models, and/or significant professional development in teaching. Experience with placing and/or supervising trainees in clinical or counseling settings. Ability to collaborate with other units and colleagues within the university and the community. Experience seeking and/or securing grant funding. Active participation in professional organizations. Experience in developing, implementing, and building sustainable collaborative relationships with local mental health facilities, counseling centers, and related organizations. Strong communication and interpersonal skills. Additional Information:  The Department of Psychology offers two undergraduate degrees, a B.A. with a concentration in psychological services and a B.S. with a concentration in psychological sciences, as well as a 4+1 joint undergraduate and Master’s degree (JUMP). In addition, the department offers the M.S. in Experimental Psychology with an optional concentration in Industrial/Organizational Psychology, and a Ph.D. in Applied Experimental Psychology with concentrations in Human Factors and Applied Experimental Psychology (Ph.D. students minor in quantitative psychology). The department is also preparing to offer a M.A. in Clinical Mental Health Counseling. Psychology department faculty focus on direct mentoring of undergraduate and graduate students. Faculty teach courses in many areas of psychology, including clinical, experimental, social, developmental, cognitive, biological, perceptual, and industrial/organizational, in addition to courses cross-listed with engineering. Rank and Salary: This is a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor. Salary will be competitive and commensurate with rank, experience, and qualifications. An excellent and comprehensive benefits package is available. Appointment Date:  Anticipated start date is Fall 2027. About the College: The College of Arts, Humanities, and Social Sciences at The University of Alabama in Huntsville has nine departments, supporting sixteen bachelor’s degree programs, three master’s programs, one Ph.D. program, and five certificate programs at the undergraduate and graduate level. Our college is housed across three separate buildings on campus, including the newly refurbished Morton Hall, with state-of-the-art facilities for teaching, research and creative activity. Our class sizes are relatively small, and our faculty are committed to students’ success. Applicants may find more information about the College, the University, and Huntsville on the College’s website . About the University: Founded in 1969, The University of Alabama in Huntsville is one of America’s premier doctoral-granting, comprehensive universities known for addressing some of the world’s most critical technological challenges. UAH, a part of the University of Alabama System, offers an environment where students are able to explore, discover, create and communicate knowledge. UAH has eight colleges, more than 100 areas of study, and 17 high-tech research centers and institutes. Located in the heart of the Technology Hub of the South, UAH serves as the anchor tenant of the second-largest research park in the United States, Cummings Research Park. UAH boasts storied partnerships with Redstone Arsenal, NASA and the Department of Defense. The institution is a member of the Gulf South athletic conference. UAH is where technology and human understanding converge to prepare students to transform their future. Learn more about the University on the UAH website. About Huntsville: Defense, space, telecommunications, biotechnology, diversified manufacturing and a variety of emerging specialties provide challenging work in Huntsville, also known as the Rocket City. The area enjoys a favorable cost of living and quality of life in the Southeast. More than 1.2 million people reside in the Huntsville metro area, and it is now the largest city in Alabama. From outdoor recreational activities to an enhanced arts and entertainment community, Huntsville contributes to a wonderful way to live, work, and play.  More information about our city is available on the City of Huntsville website . Application Procedure and Deadline: Review of applications will begin on November 30, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Interested candidates should submit a letter of interest (relating your experiences to the required/preferred qualifications and responsibilities of the position), curriculum vitae, unofficial transcripts, research statement, teaching philosophy, and evidence of teaching effectiveness. Three letters of recommendation will be requested for finalists. Please send application materials to: Beth Hodges, Staff Assistant, Departments of Psychology and Sociology, at ejh0028@uah.edu . Please reference log number 27-28-425 when applying. Please direct any questions related to the position to Dr. Dan Morrison, Interim Chair, Department of Psychology, drm0047@uah.edu (Chair of Selection Committee), or Beth Hodges, ejh0028@uah.edu."
    }
